= Introduction =
This is the Minimum intrusion Grid (MiG or migrid) project code developed and
maintained at GitHub

https://github.com/ucphhpc/migrid-sync

MiG is Free and Open Source Software originally designed and developed by the
MiG Team lead by Brian Vinter (currently brian DOT vinter AT au DOT dk).
Development and maintenance continues with the Science HPC Center at University
of Copenhagen taking over since Brian left UCPH.

Please refer to the COPYING file in this directory for further information
about the GPL license in version 2 or later under which MiG is distributed.


= Getting Started =
Please refer to the information available at the aforementioned URL
especially the wiki pages including:

https://github.com/ucphhpc/migrid-sync/wiki


= Requirements =
A MiG site basically requires an Apache web server and a Python interpreter
with a few external modules.
-Apache HTTP Server 2.4+ with bundled modules (http://httpd.apache.org/)
-Python 3 (https://www.python.org/)

The migrid code can most easily be deployed using the docker-migrid wrapping

https://github.com/ucphhpc/docker-migrid

using the instructions linked there.

Since 2026 we only support Python 3.x in all development and releases. The old
stable Python 2.x version is no longer maintained but archived in the
legacy-python2 branch.
We generally strive to keep all code compatible with long-term support releases
of the popular RHEL/Rocky and Ubuntu/Debian Linux distributions and in
particular the default python versions they provide. We do not really want or
have the resources to maintain our own complete python stack, so we also rely
on libraries and components packaged in those distributions whenever possible.
In cases where no such packages or only severely outdated ones exist we review
and rely on the best alternative public sources we can find on the Python
Package Index (PyPI) or GitHub.

== Optional Feature Requirements ==
A more comprehensive list of optional features or external service
integration include:
 - WSGI optimized web provider
 - OpenID 2.0 / Connect authentication
 - Efficient (SFTP/FTPS/WebDAVS) data access services
 - Multi-Factor Authentication (TOTP)
 - Password strength testing
 - Cracking prevention and rate limiting
 - Scheduled and event-based user actions
 - Background data transfers
 - Collaborative Wiki / SCM / Issue tracker
 - Grid jobs and resources
 - Job workflow features
 - Archives with DOI support
 - Notification services
 - Quota and accounting
 - Site operator assistant
 - LetsEncrypt server certificates
 - Interactive analysis and visualization (Jupyter)
 - Cloud integration (OpenStack)
 - File synchronization (Seafile)
 - Native or containerized unit testing
 - PDF generation
 - Instant messaging
 - JSONRPC access
 - Spell checking

The software needed for the above optional components is mainly provided by:
-Apache WSGI module (https://code.google.com/p/modwsgi/)
